        ":question/problem": "The inequality $-\\sqrt{2}<x<\\sqrt{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"
